          "ok": true,
          "tool": "read_doc",
          "content": {
            "path": "doc/event.md",
            "preview": "### Annual Engagement & Operations Review: A Strategic Briefing on Event Portfolio Performance\n\nThis document provides a comprehensive overview of the strategic units comprising our event portfolio for the preceding academic year. Each asset is analyzed based on its operational designation and engagement profile, offering leadership a granular perspective on programmatic execution.\n\n\nAn assessment of athletic engagement begins with Strategic Unit rec2N69DMcrqN9PJC, formally designated as the Women's Soccer event. This initiative is critical for fostering school spirit and has shown consistent audience draw in previous years. The post-event survey indicated a high degree of satisfaction with the new digital ticketing system, which was a notable operational improvement. The successful execution of this event underscores the viability of our current venue partnership, despite ongoing discussions about stadium lighting upgrades.\n\nFollowing this, operational asset rec0Si5cQ4rJRVzd6, the March Meeting, served as a key Q1 touchpoint for general membership. The agenda for this meeting was heavily focused on upcoming budgetary allocations. It was noted that the audiovisual equipment in the designated conference room required an unscheduled maintenance check just prior to the session, causing a minor delay. This session's success hinged on pre-circulated materials, which were instrumental in facilitating a productive discussion.\n\nThe leadership calendar commenced with Strategic Unit rec0akZnLLpGUloLH, identified as the Officers meeting - January. This inaugural leadership convocation of the calendar year set the strategic tone for the spring semester. This particular meeting is crucial for aligning officer objectives and delegating initial tasks. The catering vendor for this session received mixed reviews, particularly regarding the vegetarian options provided.\n\nThe democratic process was upheld through initiative rec0dZPcWXF0QjNnE. Initiall